---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import Updates from '../components/Updates.astro';
---
<Layout title="About the Festival | Breck Sky Fest" description="Breckenridge Sky Festival is a Summit County dark-sky celebration organized by AstroTours.org, with grant support from the Town of Breckenridge. November 2026.">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Meet Breck Sky Fest" title="Breck looks up." description="A Summit County dark-sky celebration centered in Breckenridge, bringing together astronomy, hands-on science, and local creativity in November 2026." accent="orange" icon="planet"/>
  <section class="section paper-section"><div class="wrap feature-split"><div><span class="eyebrow">The first edition</span><h2>A festival<br/>built on curiosity.</h2><p class="large">AstroTours.org organizes Breck Sky Fest with grant support from the Town of Breckenridge. The first festival runs November 11–30, 2026, with events in Breckenridge and Frisco.</p><p>Local partners bring school astronomy, free community stargazing, guided hikes, art, and family science to the festival.</p><a class="text-link" href="/program/">See the festival schedule <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div><figure class="editorial-photo"><img src="/images/friends-telescope.webp" alt="A group smiling beside an AstroTours telescope at an evening program" width="1280" height="960" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Good company at a previous AstroTours program.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
  <section class="section dark-section"><div class="wrap intro-split"><div><span class="eyebrow">Why the night sky?</span><h2>There’s a lot<br/>worth looking up for.</h2></div><div><p class="large">You don’t need a science background to be curious about a planet, a distant star, or the light that reaches us from another galaxy.</p><p>The program connects telescope viewing with hands-on science, creative workshops, and opportunities to learn about the night sky.</p><p>Dark skies are part of the festival’s focus, too. We want guests to experience the night and learn how thoughtful lighting can help protect our view of it.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section paper-section"><div class="wrap feature-split"><figure class="editorial-photo"><img src="/images/evening-telescope.webp" alt="A large telescope ready for an AstroTours evening session beside a warmly lit building" width="1280" height="960" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>A telescope setup from a previous AstroTours program.</figcaption></figure><div><span class="eyebrow">The people behind the telescopes</span><h2>Organized by<br/>AstroTours.org.</h2><p>Breck Sky Fest is led by astronomer Luke Huxley and AstroTours.org, which brings guided telescope experiences to communities, schools, visitors, and private groups in Colorado.</p><p>AstroTours leads the telescope programs and works with local partners on school visits and community events.</p><div class="actions"><a href="https://www.astrotours.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="button button-dark">Meet AstroTours.org <Icon name="diagonal" size={18}/></a><a href="/get-involved/" class="text-link">Get involved <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div></div></div></section>
  <Updates/>
</Layout>
